<article class="env-lemma" id="0AWX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0AWX">Lemma <span data-tag="0AWX">47.21.3</span>.</a> A regular local ring is Gorenstein. A regular ring is Gorenstein. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $A$ be a regular ring of finite dimension $d$. Then $A$ has finite global dimension $d$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00OE" data-tag="00OE">10.110.8</a>. Hence $\mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^{d + 1}_ A(M, A) = 0$ for all $A$-modules $M$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/065R" data-tag="065R">10.109.8</a>. Thus $A$ has finite injective dimension as an $A$-module by More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0A5T" data-tag="0A5T">15.71.2</a>. It follows that $A[0]$ is a dualizing complex, hence $A$ is Gorenstein by the remark following the definition. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>